 "faq_text": "Do I Need to Undress for Chair Massage? No. You stay fully clothed, and I do not use oil. Light layers are easiest to work through; a thick coat or a bulky hood is better taken off. How Long Is a Chair Massage? At corporate and group events I plan about 10 to 15 minutes per person, depending on the schedule and how many people take part. The Corporate and Group Massage page explains how the time block is planned. The studies summarized below mostly used 15-minute sessions. What Is the Massage Chair Like? It is a padded folding chair that you sit on facing forward, with your knees on supports, your chest against a cushion and your face in a cradle, so your back, neck and shoulders are easy to reach. I adjust it to your height. If kneeling or leaning forward is uncomfortable, I can work with you seated upright in an ordinary chair. Is Chair Massage the Same as a Mechanical Massage Chair? No. A massage chair is a machine with rollers. Chair massage is hands-on work by a licensed massage therapist in a portable chair, adapted to you as you give feedback. How Much Pressure Will You Use? Enough to reach the tight muscles, and no more than you want. Tell me what you like, and ask me to lighten up or press more firmly at any time; I will adjust. How Is Chair Massage Different From Your Other Sessions? Chair massage is seated, clothed and short, and it focuses on the back, shoulders, neck, arms and hands. Therapeutic massage and deep tissue massage take place on a table, last longer and can cover the whole body. Thai massage is done on a mat and adds assisted stretching. Each is described on its own page. Can I Have a Chair Massage If I’m Pregnant or Have a Health Condition? Often yes, with changes. Tell me before I start about any health conditions, medications, pregnancy, recent injury or surgery. I may adjust the work, leave an area out, or suggest you check with your clinician first. Will I Feel the Results Right Away? People respond differently to massage, so I can’t promise a particular result or timeline. I will ask how the session feels and adjust as I go. What Does the Host Need to Provide? A quiet corner with roughly six feet by six feet of floor space, somewhere for people to wait, and a way for them to sign up for a time. I bring the chair and everything else. The Corporate and Group Massage page lists what I plan with you before the event. Where Do Sessions Take Place? Right now I offer corporate and group massage only, at suitable Portland venues, with bookings beginning November 14, 2026. Individual appointments at a dedicated Thai Solitude location are planned for summer 2027. I do not offer individual house calls. How Do I Book a Session? Corporate and group massage bookings begin November 14, 2026. The Corporate and Group Massage page explains how to request a quote. Individual appointments are planned for summer 2027.",
 "before_text": "Chair Massage in Portland What Is Chair Massage? Chair massage is a short massage you receive sitting up, fully clothed, in a padded portable chair that supports your chest, arms and head as you lean forward. I work through your clothing without oil, mostly on the back, shoulders, neck, arms and hands. A session usually lasts 10 to 20 minutes. The chair folds up and fits in a quiet corner, which is why chair massage is what I bring to employee appreciation days and workplace events. Most research on chair massage comes from small studies in workplaces and hospitals, usually with 15-minute sessions. In 52 employees, a 15-minute chair massage at work was followed by lower blood pressure, although the study had no comparison group. [1] In 38 nurses offered a weekly 15-minute chair massage for 10 weeks, perceived-stress scores were lower at the end. [2] In a randomized trial of 60 women on oncology nursing teams, 15-minute chair massages twice a week for three weeks were followed by lower stress scores, and less interference from pain in daily life, than no massage. [3] Studies of office workers given short chair massages over a month report less neck and back discomfort, [4] [5] and one found a higher tolerance for pressure on tender muscle points. [5] These studies are small and short. I summarize them, including the weaker results, in the research section below. Chair massage is hands-on work by a licensed massage therapist, adapted to you as you give feedback. It is not a mechanical massage chair, and studies of those devices are not included here. Common Reasons Clients Choose Chair Massage People often choose chair massage when hours at a desk, on the phone or on their feet leave the neck, shoulders and upper back tight, and they want attention to those areas without changing clothes or taking an hour away from work. Others like it as a pause in a busy day, or as a first massage, since you stay dressed and the session is short. Employers book it for appreciation days and events, where each person takes a short turn in the chair. Research on chair massage for desk work points the same way, with limits. In 50 office workers, half had eight 15-minute chair massages during work breaks over four weeks and half had none; the massage group reported less pain in the upper and lower spine and the right arm, with smaller improvements in the right shoulder and left forearm, and tolerated more pressure on the muscle points tested before it hurt, most of all in the trapezius and supraspinatus muscles, while changes in the control group were not statistically significant. [5] In 124 computer workers randomly split among four weeks of chair massage, relaxing-music sessions or no intervention, the massage group had higher pressure-pain thresholds in every neck muscle tested and a higher relaxation level measured from heart-rate variability; the music group improved on relaxation but its pain thresholds were unchanged, falling in one muscle, and changes in the no-intervention group were negligible. [6] In 19 women in administration and management who had chair massage twice a week for a month, neck and upper-back discomfort scores fell and the neck bent further to the side and backward, although all 19 received the massage, with no separate control group, and only 15 completed the study. [4] Before I begin, I ask about your preferences and anything I should know about your health. You can request lighter pressure, a different focus, or a pause at any time. Who Can Benefit From Chair Massage? Chair massage suits people who want focused attention on the back, neck and shoulders in a short session, and groups where many people will take a turn. You do not need to undress or to have had a massage before, and you can ask me to stay with one area or to work more lightly. If you have an ongoing health condition, a recent injury or surgery, or you are pregnant, tell me at the start. I will keep the work within the scope of massage, adapt it or leave parts out, and I will tell you if I think you should check with a doctor first. If the chair itself is not comfortable for you, say so: I can adjust it or work with you seated upright in an ordinary chair. Questions About Chair Massage What To Expect During Your Appointment Before your session, tell me about any health conditions, medications, pregnancy, recent injury or surgery. I may adjust the work, or suggest you check with your clinician first. New numbness, weakness, unexplained swelling or radiating pain means you should see a doctor before massage. Quick Check-In Before you sit down, I ask where you feel tension, how much pressure you like, and anything about your health I should know. At a group event this takes a minute or two. Settling Into the Chair I adjust the chair to your height and show you how to rest your chest, arms and face. If anything is uncomfortable, I change it before I begin. Chair Massage Work I work through your clothing on the back, shoulders, neck, arms and hands, starting with broad pressure and spending more time where you asked. You can ask for a change at any point. Before You Go At the end, I ask how the work felt, and you can take a moment before standing up.",
